@cc [owner:Nils-Fedrigo,label:product] slack-text-extractable
Translated Slack text MUST be rendered with `i18n._("<English text>", values)` on an `I18n` instance
returned by `getSlackI18n` (`lib/i18n.ts`), held in a binding named `i18n`, never with the global
`i18n` of `@lingui/core`. The first argument MUST be a string literal, which is both the message id
and its English text: `lingui extract` only collects literal ids passed to a callee named `i18n._`.
Dynamic parts MUST be named ICU placeholders (`{agentName}`) passed in `values`, never concatenated
or interpolated into the literal. Lingui macros (`@lingui/*/macro`) MUST NOT be imported: connectors
runs under `tsx`, which does not compile them.

@cc [owner:Nils-Fedrigo,label:product] slack-user-text-translated
Text the Slack bot writes for Slack users (messages, ephemerals, assistant statuses, plan titles,
modals, buttons, placeholders, and `SlackExternalUserError` messages) MUST be translated as
specified by `slack-text-extractable`. Content not authored by the bot (agent answers, agent and
tool names, front API error messages, Slack data) MUST be interpolated untranslated through
placeholders. The `message` of other `Error`s is diagnostic: it MAY stay English and MUST only reach
users through the placeholder of a translated sentence. Text sent to the Dust API or to models
(message content, content fragments) MUST NOT be translated.

@cc [owner:Nils-Fedrigo,label:product] slack-text-in-recipient-locale
Slack text MUST be rendered with the `I18n` that `lib/user_locale.ts` returns for the Slack user it
is written to, `getSlackI18nForUser`. Text with no single human recipient (channel announcements,
replies to Slack workflows) MUST use the workspace locale, from `getSlackI18nForUser` with a `null`
recipient. Production code MUST NOT pick a locale
with `getSlackI18n` directly.
